WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.090
Close your eyes and pray for salvation: We’re
returning to the lanky, lethal, lad whose

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:08.830
rampages often result in legendary losses
of life.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.590
It’s SCP-096, the Shy Guy.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:15.450
Long-time fans of the channel will no doubt
know this anomalous monster like the back

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.250
of their hand - after all, we have several
videos on the subject that are definitely

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:23.280
worth checking out sometime - but to get the
full benefit of today’s tale, we’ll do

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.130
a quick refresh for any potential newcomers
in the audience.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.339
Let’s start with the basics: The first thing
you’ll probably notice about 096 is its

00:00:32.339 --> 00:00:35.989
daunting size and rather unusual bodily proportions.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:40.280
While mostly humanoid in shape, the Shy Guy
has long, dangling arms that almost reach

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.510
the ground.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.479
Its body is thin and hairless, with opaque,
clear white pupil-less eyes and a wide, gaping

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:47.479
mouth.

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:52.100
It’s a truly chilling sight to behold, an
abomination that conjures up nightmares just

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:57.049
to look at it… and we mean that literally,
you don’t want to look at this thing, and

00:00:57.049 --> 00:00:59.850
we’ll get to why in a moment.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:03.300
SCP-096 doesn’t really do much during an
average day.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:08.570
Usually, this tall, spindly humanoid entity
will spend its downtime just aimlessly pacing

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.970
around in its containment cell, and perhaps
occasionally taking a nap.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.600
Pretty relatable, right?

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.600
Especially if you’ve been trapped in your
college dorm room during the lockdown.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:24.920
But, if some unlucky soul should dare to look
at SCP-096 - specifically, its horrible face

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.390
- that’s when the monster starts to act
very differently.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:34.031
Now, someone doesn’t have to be directly
looking at SCP-096 to make this happen, even

00:01:34.031 --> 00:01:38.440
glancing at a blurry, distorted photo of it,
or watching security footage of the creature

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.600
can have the same deadly result.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.970
You see, the moment the SCP-096’s face is
seen, the wiry monster will know.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.180
When this happens, the creature suffers what
appears to be a psychological meltdown, covering

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.430
its face with its huge, claw-like hands and
weeping.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.640
Hence the nickname, Shy Guy.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.200
Utterly inconsolable and suffering from extreme
emotional distress, eventually the creature

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.570
will be unable to take it anymore.

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:08.709
Standing up, SCP-096 will then travel towards
whoever dared to look at it, using anomalous

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:12.489
speeds to hunt down the person that saw its
face.

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:18.489
The Foundation has proven that it is unquestionably
impossible for anyone to hide from SCP-096.

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:23.760
Literally nothing can stand in its path as
it tracks down its victim; not walls or locked

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.459
doors, even being aboard a boat in the middle
of the ocean, or in a plane in mid-air can

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:29.849
prevent it from finding you.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:35.150
And when it does find you, it’s not a pleasant
sight.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.799
SCP-096 will kill the person that looked at
it without leaving any detectable trace on

00:02:38.799 --> 00:02:39.849
their body.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:43.980
The monster is impervious to gunfire and all
other forms of conventional weaponry.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.590
It can’t be killed or even so much as harmed.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.940
Even if the Shy Guy is only seen as a tiny
little speck in a photograph, this will be

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.999
enough to trigger the creature’s relentless
defense mechanism.

00:02:54.999 --> 00:02:59.440
So, because of this, the SCP Foundation’s
only real way to keep the creature safely

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.439
locked up is in an airtight steel cube, with
no security cameras or surveillance tools

00:03:04.439 --> 00:03:05.849
of any kind.

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:10.480
All members of personnel are reminded that
they are to never, ever look at it.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:11.849
But what if they could?

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:18.239
What if, somehow, someone was able to neutralize
SCP-096’s anomalous abilities?

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:22.669
Or was resistant to being killed when the
creature inevitably tracked them down?

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:25.079
This is how the tale known as “Picture Perfect”
begins.

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:26.459
“What do you think boys?

00:03:26.459 --> 00:03:28.760
Is she a beauty or what?”

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.309
Doctor Clef proclaimed to a group of his fellow
high-ranking Foundation researchers.

00:03:33.309 --> 00:03:38.389
The group looked in confusion and unease at
what Clef had been referring to, namely a

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:41.309
photograph that he’d just hung up on the
wall of his office.

00:03:41.309 --> 00:03:43.190
A photo of what, though?

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:49.030
Well, a four-foot by five-foot picture of
none other than SCP-096, of course.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:53.000
Naturally, the other researchers shared a
mixture of shock and dismay at what Clef had

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.799
just done, as you would if one of your co-workers
hung up a photo of a creature that could kill

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:00.580
you just for looking at that said photo.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.710
We’ve lost a number of staff members from
around the SCP Explained office that exact

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:09.330
same way.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:13.379
Doctor Clef had long been one of the more
enigmatic and mysterious scientists working

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:18.310
at the Foundation, with the exception of the
elusive and infamous O5 Council.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.750
Alto Clef was formerly an operative for the
Global Occult Coalition, as well as being

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:27.250
known to be a bit of a gun enthusiast and
all-around crazy wildcard.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:31.910
Clef earned his own brand of infamy for his
rumored practice of brutally “decommissioning”

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:36.810
dangerous SCPs, and you can probably guess
what we mean by that.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:41.940
He’s the Foundation’s go-to executioner
and a specialist in dealing with reality-bending

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:42.940
anomalies.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.230
Would you expect any less from an ex-GOC?

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:50.560
Joining Clef in his office were the likes
of Doctor Jack Bright, another infamous Foundation

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.560
researcher.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:56.990
Doctor Bright, also known as SCP-963-2, was
assigned to conduct experiments on an anomalous

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:03.870
amulet, SCP-963, which resulted in his death
at the hands of SCP-076, Able.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.460
However, anomalous properties of the amulet
gave Bright the ability to inhabit new bodies

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.980
after his death.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.580
Specifically, anyone who also touches the
amulet, which kills the host and allows Bright

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.280
to take control of their body.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.190
While technically immortal, Doctor Bright
isn’t exactly stoked about that fact, often

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.980
seeking out death and wanting to know exactly
what it feels like.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:30.490
Also present at the unveiling of Doctor Clef’s
photo of SCP-096 was his friendly rival, Doctor

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:36.120
Kondraki, While usually gruff and anti-social,
Kondraki is an expert in perception-based

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.730
anomalies, normally called upon by the Foundation
to photograph SCPs that would otherwise be

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.030
invisible or imperceivable.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:47.170
Doctor Cimmerian, a member of the Foundation’s
Ethics Committee, was also in the room as

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.220
Doctor Clef proudly showed off his office’s
latest decoration.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:56.010
Perhaps the most outwardly strange researcher
in attendance was Kain “Pathos” Crow,

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:00.050
a scientist from the Foundation’s Research
Department who had been transformed into a

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:05.190
Labrador Retriever after an experiment that
needless to say, went a bit awry.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.080
Crow must have been barking up the wrong tree
on that one.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.740
Each of them expressed their reactions differently.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.590
Deeply exasperated, Doctor Cimmerian shook
his head and began to question his recent

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.120
decision to stop drinking.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.370
Meanwhile, Doctor Bright looked up at the
painting with a bemused tilting of his head.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:25.920
He wondered if SCP-096 would come to kill
him, and if it did, would it only do so once,

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:30.970
or would the creature try again and again
each time that Bright inhabited a new body?

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:34.950
Perhaps in an attempt to look away from the
photo, Kain lowered his head and covered his

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:40.060
snout with a paw, while Doctor Kondraki hung
his head in deep disappointment.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:45.150
The only one among the group that seemed unaffected
by the sight of the SCP-096 photograph was

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:49.750
Doctor Charles Gears, another high-ranking
SCP Foundation scientist.

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:55.200
It was Gears who finally broke the uncomfortable
silence, asking Doctor Clef the one question

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.580
that had been on everyone’s minds: why?

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:03.620
Clef excitedly exclaimed that the photo was
meant to be in celebration of SCP-096 finally

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.150
having been neutralized, and according to
the scientist, they were free to look at the

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.750
creature’s “ugly mug” all they wanted.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:14.250
The group stared in horror at the gaping mouth,
the screaming expression of the monster that

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.360
had killed so many people.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.480
Not one of them shared in Doctor Clef’s
enthusiasm.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:24.020
Some, like Bright and Gears, simply argued
that the picture was creepy, while others

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.240
voiced far greater concerns.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:32.330
Kain argued that putting up a photo of SCP-096
was in poor taste, that it was massively disrespectful

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:36.500
to all the unwitting victims that the Shy
Guy had killed over the years.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.300
It was also reckless and dangerous, Doctor
Cimmerian added.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:44.889
It had not yet been a full twenty four hours
since SCP-096’s apparent death, and if the

00:07:44.889 --> 00:07:49.260
creature was somehow able to come back to
life, it could easily kill every researcher

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.480
in the office.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.270
By showing them this so-called masterpiece,
Clef had put all his co-workers’ lives at

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:56.270
risk.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.930
He shrugged off their concerns of the creature
coming back to life, despite knowing that

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:01.710
was entirely possible.

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:05.960
After all, it wasn’t just anomalies and
SCPs that could defy death, most of the Foundation’s

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.300
higher ranked staff were also capable of the
same.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.130
Even some of the researchers in that very
room had died at least once.

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:18.290
Doctor Clef claimed that, should the worst
happen and SCP-096 return to life, they could

00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:21.040
simply forget its face with amnestics.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:26.560
Clef claimed that they were now free of SCP-096’s
anomalous powers, thanks to the creature’s

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:27.560
termination.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.690
But the four men and one dog in his office
with him failed to share his enthusiasm.

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:37.940
The group then began debating why the Foundation
even needed to neutralize SCP-096 in the first

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.840
place, when its containment purely hinged
on nobody ever looking at the creature.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.850
“He could be contained by putting a sack
over his head,” Doctor Bright pointed out,

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.730
met with murmurs of agreement from the others.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.270
“That’s where you’re wrong Jack.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:52.810
There could be photos of him from before he
was contained, or someone could get a hold

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.990
of the photos we already have of him,” Doctor
Clef argued back.

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:59.820
“If they did something like hack Markiplier’s
Youtube account and put that photo on his

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.330
homepage, it’d be game over.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.390
The Shy Guy would breach containment with
millions of dash ones to kill all over the

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:10.660
world, and that kind of rampage would guarantee
he’d have cameras pointed on him 24/7.

00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:15.220
Exposure would grow exponentially until the
bastard slaughtered most of civilization.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.650
As far as I'm concerned, he should have been
classified as Keter from the start.”

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:24.150
But Bright argued back, stating that the Foundation
had no real cause to kill SCP-096.

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:28.340
They already had a clear understanding that
the Shy Guy’s anomalous behaviors were only

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.720
even triggered by somebody observing it, and
preventing that from happening was pretty

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.720
easy.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.250
Killing the creature just seemed excessive,
cruel even.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:42.430
Cimmerian chimed in again, saying that killing
SCP-096 was something the Global Occult Coalition

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:47.090
would do, and the Foundation still hadn’t
learned all they could about 096.

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:51.010
Could someone survive looking at the creature
if they were in space, or another dimension?

00:09:51.010 --> 00:09:52.710
Now no one would ever know.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:58.040
On top of that, Doctor Gears pointed out that
the Foundation had no clue what SCP-096’s

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.730
exact origin was; where the creature had come
from, if it was the only one of its kind or

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.500
whether it was part of a species of similar
monsters.

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:11.350
If more beings like the Shy Guy could exist
in the future, they could cause a global catastrophe.

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:15.730
But despite all the points raised by his colleagues,
Doctor Clef didn’t want to hear any of what

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:16.730
they had to say.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.140
“Alright, I hear what you're all saying,
but shut up, you're wrong!

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:24.080
The Shy Guy was a Sword of Damocles dangling
over the whole world, we killed him, and we're

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.460
going to celebrate that by doing the one thing
we never could while he was alive; looking

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:33.930
at his face, each and every day, for the rest
of our lives, so that we never forget what

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.290
we've won and could have lost…”

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.660
The four researchers and the one who had been
turned into a dog all took a moment to consider

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:44.480
Clef’s argument - more courtesy than he
had extended to them.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.300
They all looked up at the haunting, grotesque
face of SCP-096 once more.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.440
As much as they protested the decision to
have the creature killed, each of them secretly

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:59.050
hoped it stayed neutralized, especially now
that Clef was using SCP-096’s photo as an

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.200
office decoration.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:05.070
If the creature ever managed to come back,
anyone that set foot in Clef’s office and

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:07.720
looked at the picture was as good as dead.

00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:12.260
Finally, Doctor Kondraki spoke up and threatened
to reassign Clef to one of the worst positions

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:17.380
imaginable: Critiquing first drafts of SCP
reports by junior researchers.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.391
For a moment, Clef thought his rival was bluffing,
but eventually decided it was better to be

00:11:21.391 --> 00:11:22.910
safe than sorry.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:26.881
As the gung-ho Foundation scientist begrudgingly
pulled the photo from his office wall, he

00:11:26.881 --> 00:11:30.529
muttered to the group “None of you appreciate
post-modern art.”

00:11:30.529 --> 00:11:37.380
Now go check out “SCP-096 Shy Guy ESCAPE
- Incident 096-1-A Containment Breach” and

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.550
“SCP-096 - Look at a Picture of Shy Guy
in Space?

00:11:40.550 --> 00:11:44.100
The Shy Guy Questions and Theories” for
more on the legendary Shy Guy!

